<worksheet xml:id="activity-exponential-10">
  <title>Exponential Regression: GVSU Tuition Rates</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Historical block tuition charges at GVSU are shown below. These are the actual
      tuition costs; unlike a credit card, which carries one fixed interest rate,
      tuition has grown by a different rate every year (see for yourself by checking
      the table).
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-tuition-regression">
      <title>GVSU Tuition Rates</title>

      <introduction>
        <tabular>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Fall Semester</cell>
            <cell>Block charge ($) for one year of tuition, in-state students</cell>
          </row>
          <row><cell>1995</cell><cell>2780</cell></row>
          <row><cell>1996</cell><cell>2866</cell></row>
          <row><cell>1997</cell><cell>3288</cell></row>
          <row><cell>1998</cell><cell>3362</cell></row>
          <row><cell>1999</cell><cell>4048</cell></row>
          <row><cell>2000</cell><cell>4230</cell></row>
          <row><cell>2001</cell><cell>4660</cell></row>
          <row><cell>2002</cell><cell>5056</cell></row>
          <row><cell>2003</cell><cell>5254</cell></row>
          <row><cell>2004</cell><cell>5782</cell></row>
          <row><cell>2005</cell><cell>6220</cell></row>
          <row><cell>2006</cell><cell>6588</cell></row>
          <row><cell>2007</cell><cell>7240</cell></row>
          <row><cell>2008</cell><cell>8196</cell></row>
        </tabular>
      </introduction>

      <task xml:id="task-tuition-plot" workspace="4in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            When working with yearly data, calculations are easier if we count
            forward from the starting year, so let <m>x =</m> years since 1995
            (so 1995 corresponds to <m>x = 0</m>). Plot the data below, using
            <m>x =</m> years since 1995 and <m>y =</m> tuition (dollars), and label
            your axes appropriately.
          </p>
          <figure>
            <caption>Blank grid for plotting tuition data.</caption>
            <image>
              <shortdescription>A blank coordinate grid for plotting tuition data.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>
                  A blank coordinate grid spanning 13 units horizontally and 6.5 units
                  vertically, with light minor gridlines every half unit and darker major
                  gridlines every 2.5 units, for students to plot years since 1995 against
                  tuition in dollars.
                </p>
              </description>
              <latex-image>
                <![CDATA[
                \begin{tikzpicture}
                  \draw[step=0.5cm, black!20, very thin] (0,0) grid (13,6.5);
                  \draw[step=2.5cm, black!45, thin] (0,0) grid (13,6.5);
                  \draw[black!60, thick] (0,0) rectangle (13,6.5);
                \end{tikzpicture}
                ]]>
              </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="task-tuition-equation" workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use regression to find an exponential function that best fits the data.
          </p>
          <p>
            <term>Desmos Tip:</term> Enter the data as a list, then type
            <m>y_1 \sim a(b)^{x_1}</m> below it. Check the "Log Mode" box next to the
            regression to see how well the model fits.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="task-tuition-percent" workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Based on your equation, what was the percent increase in tuition each
            year from 1995 to 2008?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="task-tuition-predict" workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use your equation to predict the block tuition rate for in-state
            students in Fall 2016.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="task-tuition-drift" workspace="1.75in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Your prediction was built from data collected between 1995 and 2008.
            The actual block rate for Fall 2016 was $11,520. What does this tell you
            about how the percent increase in tuition changed between 2008 and 2016?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="task-tuition-reflection" workspace="1.75in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            You should now be able to explain, in one sentence each: what it means
            to fit an exponential regression to data, how to convert the growth
            factor <m>b</m> into a percent increase, and why a model built on older
            data can drift away from reality over time.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>